WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.098
Where your changes live. Saving a file is not saving it in Git.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.703
You create about.html, edit index.html, and press Ctrl+S.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:16.033
Both files are saved. Git has stored nothing.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.959
Your project has three rooms, and a fourth copy far away.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:27.579
The working directory holds the files you edit. The staging area holds the next commit, being prepared.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:35.166
The repository holds the commits already saved. The fourth room, the remote, is GitHub. It stays dark for now.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.810
At every step, one command asks the same question: where is everything?

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:49.871
git status. index.html is modified. about.html is untracked: Git has never stored it.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.649
Both still sit in the working directory.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.269
git add, then both names. Git copies a snapshot of each file into the staging area.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.532
Now git diff shows nothing. The changes are not lost. They are staged.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.052
Edit a file after adding it, and the staged copy stays the old one. Add it again.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.180
That is the point: the staging area holds exactly what the next commit will contain.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.855
git diff compares the working directory with the staging area: edits not added yet.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.964
git diff --staged compares the staging area with the last commit.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.646
That is what a commit would record right now. Read it before you commit.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.120
git commit, with a message. Everything staged is sealed into a commit.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.993
A commit is a complete snapshot of the project, named by a hash.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.684
It also points to its parent, the commit before it.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:55.051
Nothing is staged now. git status says: nothing to commit, working tree clean.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.501
git log --oneline lists the history, newest first.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.873
Each commit points to its parent. That chain is the history.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.371
Change even one letter of a message, and the hash changes. History cannot be altered silently.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.860
So far, only your laptop knows.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:20.677
git status now says: ahead of origin/main by 1 commit.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:27.782
git push uploads the commits the remote does not have yet. Only commits travel, never loose edits.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.515
Now the fourth room lights up.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.923
Practise it with the commit and branch simulator, in Version control.
